  "worked_solution": "We can compute the variance of a discrete random variable $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =0\\cdot f(0)+1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{6})+1(\\frac{1}{9})+2(\\frac{1}{3})+3(\\frac{2}{9})+4(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={0}^{2}\\cdot f(0)+{1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & =0(\\frac{1}{6})+1(\\frac{1}{9})+4(\\frac{1}{3})+9(\\frac{2}{9})+16(\\frac{1}{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{9}-{(\\frac{19}{9})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{55}{9}-\\frac{361}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{134}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
